      "text": "# Smart Complaint Routing\n\nMost campus and government complaint systems stop at \"submit complaint,\" then someone manually figures out where it belongs.\n\n\"Street light outside Block C hasn\u0027t worked for 4 days\" → Electrical maintenance · medium priority · Block C.\n\nThen track SLA and auto-escalate when it\u0027s breached.\n\n# 🛠️ Smart Complaint Routing\n\n### Current problem\n\nSomeone complains:\n\n\u003e \"Streetlight near Block C hasn\u0027t worked for 5 days.\"\n\u003e \n\nHuman/operator has to figure out:\n\n\u003e Which department?\n\u003e \n\nYour system does:\n\n```\nComplaint\n   ↓\nClassification\n   ↓\nDepartment\n   ↓\nPriority\n   ↓\nAssigned worker\n```\n\n### Example\n\n```\nComplaint:\n\"Water leaking near hostel block.\"\n\nCategory:\nPlumbing\n\nPriority:\nHigh\n\nLocation:\nHostel B\n\nAssigned:\nMaintenance Team 2\n```\n\n### Interesting addition\n\n**SLA tracking.**\n\nIf nobody handles the issue:\n\n```\n12h → reminder\n24h → escalation\n48h → higher authority\n```\n\nNow you have a workflow engine.\n\n### Strong-team version\n\nLearn from previous complaints:\n\n\u003e This type of complaint usually requires X department.\n\u003e \n\n### Difficulty\n\n4/10 → 7/10.\n\n# MVP\n\n# 🛠️ Smart Complaint Routing\n\n### Your exact MVP\n\nUser submits:\n\n\u003e \"Water leaking near hostel block.\"\n\u003e \n\nSystem produces:\n\n```\nCategory: Plumbing\nPriority: High\nLocation: Hostel B\nAssigned: Maintenance Team 2\n```\n\nThen SLA tracking:\n\n```\n12h → reminder\n24h → escalation\n48h → higher authority\n```\n\n### Feasibility: **8.5/10 for your MVP**\n\nThis is a little more complicated than Lost \u0026 Found because now you\u0027re building a **workflow system**, not merely a classifier.\n\nThe core architecture is:\n\n```\nComplaint\n    ↓\nText Processing\n    ↓\nClassification\n    ├── Department\n    ├── Category\n    ├── Priority\n    └── Location\n    ↓\nAssignment Engine\n    ↓\nTicket\n    ↓\nSLA Timer\n    ↓\nResolution / Escalation\n```\n\n---\n\n# 1. Complaint ingestion\n\nInput can simply be:\n\n```\nTitle\nDescription\nLocation\nOptional image\n```\n\nExample:\n\n\u003e Street light near Block C has not worked for 5 days.\n\u003e \n\n---\n\n# 2. Classification\n\nYou want structured output:\n\n```\n{\n  \"category\":\"street_light\",\n  \"department\":\"electrical\",\n  \"priority\":\"medium\",\n  \"location\":\"Block C\"\n}\n```\n\nI\u0027d use Gemini for initial classification.\n\nBut again:\n\n### Don\u0027t let the LLM directly control the workflow.\n\nInstead:\n\n```\nLLM\n ↓\nstructured prediction\n ↓\nbackend validation\n ↓\nassignment rules\n```\n\n---\n\n# 3. Department routing\n\nCreate a mapping table:\n\n```\ncategory            department\n\nstreet_light        Electrical\nwater_leak          Plumbing\nbroken_door         Civil/Maintenance\ninternet_issue      IT\ngarbage             Sanitation\nsecurity_issue      Security\n```\n\nThis should be deterministic.\n\nIf the LLM says:\n\n\u003e Electrical\n\u003e \n\nyour backend checks whether that is a valid department.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Priority determination\n\nYou can start with rules.\n\nFor example:\n\n```\nEmergency / safety risk → Critical\n\nMajor water leakage\nelectrical hazard\nsecurity issue\n       ↓\nHigh\n\nNormal infrastructure problem\n       ↓\nMedium\n\nMinor inconvenience\n       ↓\nLow\n```\n\nThe LLM can extract severity indicators, but the final priority can be determined by rules.\n\nThis is much safer.\n\n---\n\n# 5. Assignment engine\n\nSuppose:\n\n```\nDepartment \u003d Electrical\nLocation \u003d Hostel B\n```\n\nYour system searches:\n\n```\nworkers\nWHERE department \u003d electrical\nAND active \u003d true\n```\n\nThen rank them using:\n\n```\nlocation\ncurrent workload\navailability\nskill\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```\nTeam 1 → 4 active tickets\nTeam 2 → 1 active ticket\nTeam 3 → unavailable\n```\n\n→ Team 2.\n\nYou don\u0027t need sophisticated optimization for the MVP.\n\n---\n\n# 6. Ticket lifecycle\n\nThis is important.\n\nYour ticket shouldn\u0027t just be:\n\n```\nOPEN\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```\nOPEN\n ↓\nASSIGNED\n ↓\nACKNOWLEDGED\n ↓\nIN_PROGRESS\n ↓\nRESOLVED\n ↓\nCLOSED\n```\n\nThat gives you the basis for SLA tracking.\n\n---\n\n# 7. SLA engine\n\nEvery category gets an SLA.\n\nFor example:\n\n```\nCritical → 4 hours\nHigh     → 12 hours\nMedium   → 24 hours\nLow      → 72 hours\n```\n\nWhen a ticket is created:\n\n```\ncreated_at \u003d 10:00\nSLA \u003d 24h\n\ndeadline \u003d tomorrow 10:00\n```\n\nYour backend periodically checks:\n\n```\ncurrent_time \u003e SLA_deadline\nAND status !\u003d resolved\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```\n→ escalation\n```\n\n---\n\n# 8. Escalation hierarchy\n\nYou can model:\n\n```\nWorker\n   ↓\nSupervisor\n   ↓\nDepartment Head\n   ↓\nAdministrator\n```\n\nAnd escalation rules:\n\n```\nSLA - 2h\n→ reminder\n\nSLA breached\n→ supervisor\n\nSLA + 24h\n→ department head\n```\n\nThis is where the project becomes a genuine **workflow engine**.\n\n---\n\n# 9. Dashboard\n\n### Student view\n\n```\nMy Complaint\n\nStreetlight broken near Block C\n\nStatus: 🟠 In Progress\nDepartment: Electrical\nAssigned: Team 2\n\nExpected resolution:\nToday, 6 PM\n```\n\n### Admin view\n\n```\nOPEN TICKETS\n\nElectrical       12\nPlumbing          7\nIT                5\nSanitation        4\n\n⚠️ SLA BREACHED\n3 tickets\n```\n\nThis dual-sided UI is important.\n\nYou\u0027re solving the problem for:\n\n**complainant + institution**\n\nrather than only providing a complaint form.\n\n---\n\n# 10. Historical learning\n\nYour \"strong-team version\" is interesting.\n\nSuppose historical data contains:\n\n```\n\"street light not working\"\n→ Electrical\n```\n\nand:\n\n```\n\"light outside hostel stopped\"\n→ Electrical\n```\n\nYou can learn classification from historical tickets.\n\nBut don\u0027t train a custom model initially.\n\nUse:\n\n```\nLLM classifier\n+\nhistorical examples / few-shot examples\n```\n\nThen later:\n\n```\nhistorical ticket dataset\n ↓\nclassifier\n ↓\nevaluation\n```\n\n---\n\n# 11. Location extraction\n\nThis could become a surprisingly useful feature.\n\nComplaint:\n\n\u003e \"Water leaking outside hostel B near the north gate.\"\n\u003e \n\nExtract:\n\n```\nCampus\n→ Hostel B\n→ North Gate\n```\n\nIf your campus has a location hierarchy, the system can automatically route based on location.\n\n---\n\n# 12. Image attachment\n\nPotential future feature:\n\n\u003e Upload a photo of the damaged streetlight.\n\u003e \n\nVision model extracts:\n\n```\nObject: streetlight\nCondition: damaged\nPotential hazard: yes\n```\n\nThis could improve categorization.\n\nBut again:\n\n**not required for your MVP.**\n\n---\n\n# Recommended stack\n\n| Component | Technology |\n| --- | --- |\n| Frontend | Next.js + TypeScript |\n| Backend | FastAPI |\n| DB | PostgreSQL |\n| AI classification | Gemini |\n| Validation | Pydantic |\n| Background jobs | Celery / APScheduler |\n| Realtime dashboard | WebSockets |\n| Notifications | Firebase Cloud Messaging |\n| Auth | Firebase/Auth.js |\n\n### One architectural choice\n\nFor a hackathon, **don\u0027t create microservices**.\n\nUse:\n\n```\nNext.js\n   ↓\nFastAPI\n   ↓\nPostgreSQL\n   ↓\nBackground scheduler\n```\n\nThat\u0027s enough.\n\n---\n\n# Difficulty\n\n| Component | Difficulty |\n| --- | --- |\n| Complaint form | 1/10 |\n| Ticket database | 2/10 |\n| Classification | 3/10 |\n| Priority rules | 3/10 |\n| Assignment | 4/10 |\n| Ticket lifecycle | 3/10 |\n| SLA timers | 4/10 |\n| Escalation | 5/10 |\n| Historical learning | 6/10 |\n| Full workflow engine | 7/10 |\n\n### Your exact MVP: **8.5/10 feasible**\n\nThe **SLA + escalation** portion is what makes this more than CRUD.\n\n---\n\n# Feasibility Report\n\n# 🛠️ Smart Complaint Routing\n\n### Your MVP\n\n```\nUser complaint\n      ↓\nAI classification\n      ↓\nDepartment\nPriority\nLocation\n      ↓\nAssignment\n      ↓\nSLA timer\n      ↓\nReminder / Escalation\n```\n\nExample:\n\n\u003e \"Water leaking near Hostel B\"\n\u003e \n\nbecomes:\n\n```\nCategory: Plumbing\nDepartment: Maintenance\nPriority: High\nLocation: Hostel B\nAssigned: Team 2\n\nSLA: 12 hours\n```\n\n---\n\n## ⭐ Feasibility\n\n| Category | Score |\n| --- | --- |\n| First-year feasibility | **8.5/10** |\n| Coding difficulty | 5/10 |\n| AI difficulty | 3/10 |\n| Data requirement | **9/10** |\n| External dependency risk | **9/10** |\n| Database/backend | 5/10 |\n| Demo reliability | 9/10 |\n| SIH potential | **8.5/10** |\n| Future scope | 9/10 |\n| **Overall feasibility** | 🟢 **8.5/10** |\n\n### Verdict: 🟢 **Strong candidate**\n\nThis is probably the **most \"software-engineering\" project** of these three.\n\nUnlike the document projects, you aren\u0027t just extracting information. You\u0027re creating a **closed-loop workflow**:\n\n\u003e complaint → decision → assignment → monitoring → escalation → resolution.\n\u003e \n\nThat is good for SIH.\n\n---\n\n# What you actually need\n\n### Required\n\n- Complaint submission UI\n- PostgreSQL\n- Complaint classification\n- Department mapping\n- Priority rules\n- Worker/team database\n- Assignment logic\n- Ticket status\n- SLA calculation\n- Background scheduler\n- Admin dashboard\n\n### Optional\n\n- Gemini classification\n- Email notifications\n- Push notifications\n- Image analysis\n- Analytics\n- Historical learning\n\n---\n\n# External requirements\n\n| External thing | MVP? | Cost | Risk |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Gemini API | ✅ Recommended | 🟢 | Low |\n| PostgreSQL | ✅ | 🟢 | Low |\n| Hosting | ✅ | 🟢/🟡 | Low |\n| Email API | ❌ | 🟡 | Low |\n| SMS API | ❌ | 🔴 | Medium |\n| Maps API | ❌ | 🟢 | — |\n| Government complaint API | ❌ | 🔴 | **Very high** |\n| College backend | ❌ | 🔴 | **Very high** |\n| ML training dataset | ❌ | 🟢 | — |\n| GPU | ❌ | 🟢 | — |\n| Hardware | ❌ | 🟢 | — |\n\n### ⭐ Important\n\n**Do NOT make integration with an actual government/college complaint system a requirement for your MVP.**\n\nYou can completely demonstrate it using your own ticket database.\n\nThat\u0027s a huge advantage.\n\n---\n\n# The hidden difficulty\n\nThe complaint classifier itself is easy.\n\nThis:\n\n\u003e \"Streetlight near Block C hasn\u0027t worked for four days.\"\n\u003e \n\n→ Electrical\n\nis trivial with an LLM.\n\nThe harder part is:\n\n\u003e **What happens after classification?**\n\u003e \n\nYou need to implement:\n\n```\ndepartment\n     ↓\navailable teams\n     ↓\nworkload\n     ↓\nlocation\n     ↓\nassignment\n     ↓\nSLA\n     ↓\nescalation\n```\n\nThat\u0027s where most of your development time will go.\n\n---\n\n# Assignment logic\n\nDon\u0027t make this AI initially.\n\nCreate:\n\n```\nTeam 1\nElectrical\nBlock A\n3 active tickets\n\nTeam 2\nElectrical\nBlock C\n1 active ticket\n\nTeam 3\nElectrical\nBlock B\n5 active tickets\n```\n\nThen use a deterministic score:\n\n```\nlocation proximity\n+\ncurrent workload\n+\navailability\n```\n\n→ Team 2.\n\nYou can later say:\n\n\u003e \"Our assignment engine optimizes workload and geographical proximity.\"\n\u003e \n\nMuch better than saying:\n\n\u003e \"Gemini assigns workers.\"\n\u003e \n\n---\n\n# SLA system\n\nThis is one of the strongest parts of the idea.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nPriority       SLA\n\nCritical       4h\nHigh           12h\nMedium         24h\nLow            72h\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```\nTicket created\n      ↓\nSLA deadline calculated\n      ↓\nBackground scheduler\n      ↓\nStill unresolved?\n      ↓\nEscalate\n```\n\n### External service NOT required\n\nYou can implement this entirely with:\n\n**Python + PostgreSQL + scheduler.**\n\nYou don\u0027t need a special workflow platform.\n\n---\n\n# Biggest risk\n\n### 🔴 Overbuilding the workflow\n\nYou could easily end up trying to build:\n\n\u003e Jira + ServiceNow + AI + Maps + notifications\n\u003e \n\nin a hackathon.\n\nDon\u0027t.\n\nYour MVP should have only:\n\n```\nComplaint\n ↓\nClassification\n ↓\nAssignment\n ↓\nSLA\n ↓\nEscalation\n```\n\nThat\u0027s enough.\n\n---\n\n# What I would build for SIH\n\n### Phase 1 — absolutely required\n\n```\nStudent\n ↓\nSubmit complaint\n ↓\nAI categorizes\n ↓\nBackend validates\n ↓\nTicket created\n```\n\n### Phase 2\n\n```\nTicket\n ↓\nTeam assignment\n ↓\nAdmin dashboard\n```\n\n### Phase 3\n\n```\nSLA timer\n ↓\nBreach\n ↓\nAutomatic escalation\n```\n\n### Phase 4 if time remains\n\nAdd:\n\n- analytics\n- historical classification\n- image attachment\n- map\n- notification\n\n---\n\n# SIH demo strength\n\n**Very good.**\n\nYou can deliberately create:\n\n\u003e \"Streetlight near Block C hasn\u0027t worked for 5 days.\"\n\u003e \n\nThen show:\n\n```\nAI ANALYSIS\n\nDepartment\nElectrical\n\nPriority\nMedium\n\nLocation\nBlock C\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```\nASSIGNMENT\n\nElectrical Team 2\n\nReason:\n✓ Handles Block C\n✓ Available\n✓ Lowest workload\n```\n\nThen manipulate the ticket clock:\n\n```\n⚠️ SLA BREACHED\n\nEscalating to:\nElectrical Supervisor\n```\n\nThat\u0027s a **very strong live demo** because the judge can see the entire workflow.\n\n### Final verdict: 🟢 **Proceed**\n\nbsicaly i want a Product descrion and blupritn dont use cauual languase but not too tecnical its a grop project so keep it in small modules ",
