      "text": "# Smart Complaint Routing System — Comprehensive System Blueprint\n\n---\n\n## 1. System Overview \u0026 Core Purpose\nThe **Smart Complaint Routing System** is an end-to-end automation platform for managing facility and infrastructure issues. It eliminates manual triage by reading unstructured complaints, extracting key details, determining the correct department and priority, assigning the task to the least-burdened worker nearby, and actively enforcing resolution deadlines through automated escalation.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Step-by-Step System Workflow\n\n```\n[1. User Submission \u0026 Duplicate Check]\n                │\n                ▼\n[2. AI Extraction \u0026 Classification]\n    ├── Identifies: Department, Category, Location, Safety Risks\n    └── Flags: Ambiguous or invalid inputs\n                │\n                ▼\n[3. Deterministic Priority Assignment]\n    └── Sets Priority: Critical, High, Medium, or Low\n                │\n                ▼\n[4. Dispatch \u0026 Workload Balancing]\n    ├── Checks: Active Shift + Department Match + Zone\n    └── Assigns to: Worker with the lowest active task count\n                │\n                ▼\n[5. Ticket Creation \u0026 SLA Clock Start]\n    └── Calculates dynamic resolution deadline\n                │\n                ▼\n[6. Real-Time Tracking \u0026 Escalation Ladder]\n    ├── 80% SLA Time Elapsed  ──► Reminder alert to worker\n    ├── SLA Breached (100%)   ──► Reassigned/Alerted to Supervisor\n    └── SLA + 24 Hours Over   ──► Escalated to Department Head\n                │\n                ▼\n[7. Resolution, Verification \u0026 Auto-Closure]\n    ├── Worker submits completion note / proof photo\n    ├── Complainant confirms fix OR rejects\n    └── System closes ticket \u0026 archives audit trail\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Complaint Ingestion \u0026 Pre-Processing\n* **User Input Collection:**\n  * The user enters a title and a raw description of the issue in plain text.\n  * The user selects or types the location (e.g., Campus Zone, Building Name, Floor, or Room Number).\n  * The user can optionally upload a photo of the problem.\n* **Duplicate Detection Check:**\n  * Before creating a new ticket, the system scans existing open tickets in the exact same location.\n  * If a matching issue is already open (e.g., three people reporting the same broken water pipe in Block B), the system links the new user to the existing ticket so they receive updates without creating duplicate work orders.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Text Analysis \u0026 Data Extraction\n* The system reads the raw text submitted by the user and extracts four distinct data points:\n  1. **Primary Category:** Identifies the core problem (e.g., broken tap, power outage, garbage pileup, elevator malfunction).\n  2. **Responsible Department:** Maps the category directly to an internal department (e.g., Electrical, Plumbing, Housekeeping, Civil Maintenance, IT Support).\n  3. **Location Hierarchy:** Standardizes the location into a structured format (e.g., *East Campus → Hostel 4 → 2nd Floor Corridor*).\n  4. **Hazard \u0026 Urgency Keywords:** Scans for danger indicators (e.g., *sparks, exposed wire, flooding, smoke, gas smell, blocked emergency exit*).\n* **Fallback for Ambiguous Inputs:**\n  * If the text is nonsensical, too vague, or cannot be categorized, the system routes the ticket to a **General Help Desk** queue for quick manual review instead of failing silently.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Rule-Based Priority Determination\nTo prevent artificial intelligence errors from misjudging urgency, priority is calculated using strict, deterministic business rules:\n\n* **Critical Priority (4-Hour Resolution Limit):**\n  * Assigned if hazard flags are detected (electrical sparks, severe water flooding, structural hazards, physical security locks broken).\n* **High Priority (12-Hour Resolution Limit):**\n  * Assigned if an entire group of people is impacted or essential daily utilities are down (e.g., main water cooler broken, entire floor Wi-Fi down, washroom out of order).\n* **Medium Priority (24-Hour Resolution Limit):**\n  * Assigned to localized, standard maintenance issues (e.g., a single flickering lightbulb, a slow-draining sink, a broken chair).\n* **Low Priority (72-Hour Resolution Limit):**\n  * Assigned to non-blocking or cosmetic issues (e.g., peeling paint, minor floor scuff, faded directional signage).\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Smart Dispatch \u0026 Worker Assignment\nOnce the department and priority are established, the assignment engine selects the best worker using the following sequence:\n\n1. **Availability Filter:** Queries all workers registered under the assigned department who are currently marked as **On Shift** and **Active**.\n2. **Zone Matching:** Filters workers assigned to that specific physical zone or building. If none are available in that zone, it expands the search to the general campus pool.\n3. **Workload Assessment:** Checks how many open, unresolved tickets each candidate worker currently holds.\n4. **Final Assignment:** Assigns the ticket to the qualified worker with the **lowest active ticket count**.\n5. **Worker Notification:** Immediately pushes a task notification to the assigned worker\u0027s mobile or web portal.\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: SLA Calculation \u0026 Active Monitoring\n* As soon as the ticket is assigned, the system sets an exact timestamp for the **Resolution Deadline** based on the priority level.\n* **The Countdown Engine:**\n  * Runs continuous background checks on all active tickets.\n  * Measures the exact time elapsed since ticket creation against the deadline.\n* **Pause / Hold Conditions:**\n  * If a worker requires external replacement parts, they can request a \"Material Hold.\"\n  * A hold pauses the SLA clock only after approval from a supervisor, preventing unfair SLA breaches.\n\n---\n\n### Step 6: Multi-Tier Escalation Hierarchy\nIf a ticket remains unresolved as time progresses, the system executes an automated escalation sequence:\n\n```\n[Normal State]      Assigned Worker is working on the ticket.\n                          │\n[80% Time Passed]   System sends an urgent Reminder Alert to the Worker.\n                          │\n[100% SLA Breached] System changes ticket state to \"Escalated: Level 1\"\n                    ├── Alert sent to Department Supervisor.\n                    └── Ticket turns RED on the management dashboard.\n                          │\n[SLA + 24 Hours]    If still untouched, changes to \"Escalated: Level 2\"\n                    └── Alert sent directly to the Campus Director / Facility Head.\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 7: Resolution, Verification \u0026 Auto-Closure\n* **Worker Action:**\n  * The worker arrives on-site, inspects the problem, and updates status to `In Progress`.\n  * Once completed, the worker marks the ticket as `Resolved`, enters a brief description of the work done, and optionally uploads a photo showing the fix.\n* **User Verification:**\n  * The complainant receives a notification: *\"Your complaint has been marked as resolved. Please confirm.\"*\n  * The user has two options:\n    * **Confirm:** Ticket moves to `Closed`.\n    * **Reject / Re-open:** User states why the fix was incomplete (e.g., *\"The light turns on but flickers violently\"*). The ticket returns to `Assigned` status with an elevated priority.\n* **Auto-Closure Rule:**\n  * If the user does not respond within 48 hours of resolution, the system automatically closes the ticket and archives the audit log.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Detailed Interface Specifications\n\n### 3.1 Complainant Portal (Public / Student / Resident View)\n* **Submission Screen:** A clean form with title, text box, building dropdown, and optional image upload.\n* **Live Status Tracker:** A visual progress bar showing:\n  * `Submitted` ➔ `Assigned` ➔ `In Progress` ➔ `Resolved` ➔ `Closed`.\n* **Details Displayed:** Shows the ticket reference number, category, assigned department, and the expected resolution time.\n* **Resolution Feedback:** A simple one-click confirmation screen with star-rating feedback (1 to 5 stars) once fixed.\n\n---\n\n### 3.2 Maintenance Worker Portal\n* **Task Inbox:** A list of tickets assigned directly to the logged-in worker, automatically sorted with the most urgent SLA deadline at the top.\n* **Ticket Card Details:**\n  * Exact location with building, floor, and room details.\n  * Full problem description and user-submitted photos.\n  * A live color-coded countdown timer (Green \u003d Safe, Yellow \u003d Under 2 Hours Left, Red \u003d Overdue).\n* **Action Controls:**\n  * `Acknowledge Task` (signals that the worker is aware).\n  * `Mark In Progress` (signals that work has started on-site).\n  * `Request Hold / Parts` (requests a deadline pause from supervisor).\n  * `Mark as Resolved` (prompts for a completion note and proof photo).\n\n---\n\n### 3.3 Supervisor Dashboard (Department Level)\n* **Team Overview:** Shows all workers in that department, their current on-shift status, and their active task counts.\n* **Department Ticket Board:** A categorized Kanban-style board separating tickets by status (`New`, `Assigned`, `In Progress`, `Resolved`).\n* **Breach Monitor:** A high-visibility section displaying all tickets that have breached their SLA or are within 1 hour of breaching.\n* **Manual Override Controls:**\n  * Ability to manually reassign a ticket from an overloaded worker to a free worker.\n  * Ability to manually approve or reject material hold requests.\n\n---\n\n### 3.4 Campus Administrator / Facility Head Control Panel\n* **Institution-Wide Metrics:**\n  * Total complaints received today / this week.\n  * Percentage of complaints resolved within SLA (e.g., \"94% on-time resolution rate\").\n  * Breakdown of complaints by department (e.g., Electrical: 42%, Plumbing: 30%, IT: 18%, Sanitation: 10%).\n* **Department Accountability Table:**\n  * Lists each department, average resolution time, total breaches, and customer satisfaction ratings.\n* **Escalation Incident Log:** A complete log of every Level 2 escalation sent to high-level management, detailing who failed to resolve it and why.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Handling Edge Cases \u0026 Exception Rules\n\n* **Case 1: No Workers Available on Shift**\n  * *Action:* The ticket is marked as `Unassigned - Pending Shift`. It is placed in the department\u0027s high-priority intake queue and automatically assigned to the first worker who logs in for the next shift.\n* **Case 2: Worker Falls Ill or Goes Off-Duty Mid-Task**\n  * *Action:* When a worker toggles their status to `Off Duty`, any tickets marked `Assigned` (but not yet `In Progress`) are automatically returned to the pool and reassigned to the next available worker.\n* **Case 3: Wrong Department Assigned**\n  * *Action:* If a worker arrives and discovers the issue belongs to a different team (e.g., reported as a plumbing leak, but it is an air-conditioning drainage pipe), the worker clicks `Re-route Department`. The ticket goes directly to the correct department without making the user submit a new complaint.\n* **Case 4: Spammed or Inappropriate Content**\n  * *Action:* The AI flags offensive, abusive, or spam text and diverts it to a hidden moderation queue for admin review, preventing fake tickets from entering worker task queues.",
