      "text": "# Module F1: Application Shell \u0026 Navigation Layout — Detailed Blueprint\n\n---\n\n## 1. Module Objective\nTo build the overarching visual framework, page routing, design system, and shared UI building blocks for the entire frontend. \n\nCompleting this module first ensures that the developers working on the **Submission Form (F2)**, **Tracking Page (F3)**, and **Admin Dashboard (F4)** build inside a consistent, pre-styled frame without layout conflicts or inconsistent colors and fonts.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Page Routing \u0026 URL Architecture\n\nThe frontend shell must establish four core application routes:\n\n```\n[Main Application Router]\n    ├── Route 1:  /               ──► Public Complaint Submission Screen (Host for Module F2)\n    ├── Route 2:  /track          ──► Public Ticket Tracking Lookup Screen (Host for Module F3)\n    ├── Route 3:  /admin          ──► Internal Staff / Admin Management View (Host for Module F4)\n    └── Route 4:  /* (Fallback)   ──► Friendly \"Page Not Found\" 404 Screen\n```\n\n* **Route 1: `/` (File a Complaint)** — The default home screen where students or campus residents submit issues.\n* **Route 2: `/track` (Track a Ticket)** — The search screen where users input a tracking code to check status.\n* **Route 3: `/admin` (Staff Portal)** — The internal view where maintenance coordinators view tickets and update progress.\n* **Route 4: `*` (404 Not Found)** — A clean fallback page with a \"Return to Home\" button if an invalid URL is entered.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Global Structural Layout\n\nEvery page in the application sits inside a unified layout shell consisting of three areas:\n\n```\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                        GLOBAL TOP NAVIGATION BAR                       │\n│  [Logo + App Name]                 [File Complaint] [Track Status] [Staff]\n├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│                                                                        │\n│                       CENTRAL CONTENT CONTAINER                        │\n│                 (Dynamic Page Views: F2, F3, or F4)                    │\n│                                                                        │\n├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│                             GLOBAL FOOTER                              │\n│       [Campus Facility Portal]  •  [Support Info]  •  [System Status]  │\n└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n### 3.1 Top Navigation Bar (Header)\n* **Branding Area (Left):** Campus/Organization icon paired with the system name (*\"Campus Care / Grievance Desk\"*). Clicking this logo always routes back to `/`.\n* **Primary Navigation Links (Right):**\n  * Link 1: *\"Submit Issue\"* ➔ Points to `/`\n  * Link 2: *\"Track Status\"* ➔ Points to `/track`\n  * Link 3: *\"Staff Portal\"* ➔ Points to `/admin` (styled distinctly as a secondary button).\n* **Active Link Highlight:** The currently active page must display a distinct visual highlight (e.g., bold text and a colored underline/pill) so users always know where they are.\n* **Mobile Responsiveness:** On narrow mobile screens, collapse the navigation links into a clean slide-out menu or simple mobile header bar.\n\n### 3.2 Main Content Container\n* Centers all page content horizontally on the screen.\n* Enforces a maximum content width to prevent forms and tables from stretching awkwardly across ultra-wide desktop monitors.\n* Applies uniform top and bottom breathing room (padding) across all pages.\n\n### 3.3 Global Footer\n* Displays the organization/institution name.\n* Includes a brief support notice (e.g., *\"For emergency fire or medical hazards, please contact Campus Emergency Services directly\"*).\n* Displays a subtle \"System Operational\" status indicator.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Visual Design System \u0026 Theme Foundations\n\nTo keep the application looking unified across all pages, establish these standard design rules:\n\n### 4.1 Color Palette\n* **Background Surface:** Clean, modern neutral tone (off-white or deep slate) that prevents eye strain.\n* **Card \u0026 Container Surface:** High-contrast background with subtle outer borders to clearly separate forms and tables from the page background.\n* **Primary Action Color:** A vibrant, high-contrast action color (e.g., Deep Royal Blue or Bold Orange) used exclusively for main action buttons (Submit, Search, Save).\n* **Text Hierarchy Colors:**\n  * Primary Headings: High-contrast pure dark / pure white.\n  * Body Text: Medium-contrast readable neutral.\n  * Muted / Helper Text: Soft low-contrast neutral for timestamps and field descriptions.\n\n### 4.2 Standard Status Badge Colors\nStandardize the visual indicators used across the entire platform for ticket states:\n\n| Status Label | Visual Style | Meaning |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **`SUBMITTED`** | Amber / Yellow Pill Badge | Issue received, awaiting staff action. |\n| **`IN_PROGRESS`** | Blue / Purple Pill Badge | Maintenance team has been notified or is working. |\n| **`RESOLVED`** | Solid Green Pill Badge | Issue has been fixed and completed. |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Shared UI Component Primitives\n\nModule F1 must build and export **5 reusable base components** so developers working on F2, F3, and F4 do not duplicate UI work:\n\n1. **Standard Button Component:**\n   * Supports three visual styles: *Primary* (solid accent fill), *Secondary* (outlined/neutral), and *Danger* (red outline/fill).\n   * Includes built-in support for a *Loading Spinner State* (disables the button and shows a spinning indicator while an API request is processing).\n2. **Text Input \u0026 Textarea Component:**\n   * Standard input box with consistent rounded borders, focus outline, floating or fixed label, and a designated container beneath it for red validation error messages.\n3. **Card Container Component:**\n   * A pre-styled content box with standardized padding, rounded corners, and subtle drop shadows for wrapping forms and ticket cards.\n4. **Status Badge Pill Component:**\n   * Accepts a status word (`SUBMITTED`, `IN_PROGRESS`, `RESOLVED`) and automatically renders the appropriate color-coded pill.\n5. **Global Notification Toast / Alert Banner:**\n   * A pop-up alert that temporarily appears at the top corner of the screen to show instant feedback (e.g., *\"Complaint submitted successfully\"* or *\"Failed to connect to server\"*).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Implementation Checklist for the Assigned Developer\n\n- [ ] Initialize the base frontend project and clean out default boilerplate templates.\n- [ ] Configure global font styling (modern, highly readable sans-serif).\n- [ ] Set up the client-side router with routes for `/`, `/track`, `/admin`, and a 404 page.\n- [ ] Build the **Global Navigation Bar** with active-route highlighting and mobile toggle support.\n- [ ] Build the **Global Footer**.\n- [ ] Create the **Main Content Container** wrapper.\n- [ ] Build the **Shared UI Primitives**:\n  - [ ] Button (Primary, Secondary, Loading state).\n  - [ ] Form Input \u0026 Textarea fields with error message states.\n  - [ ] Card Surface wrapper.\n  - [ ] Status Badge Pill.\n  - [ ] Global Feedback Alert / Toast.\n- [ ] Verify that navigating between `/`, `/track`, and `/admin` transitions smoothly without visual page flickering or layout shifting.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Handoff Contract (What F2, F3, and F4 Receive)\n\nOnce Module F1 is complete:\n\n* **For Developer F2 (Submission Form):** You will build inside the `/` route. You can immediately import the pre-styled `Card`, `Input`, `Textarea`, and `Button` components without writing custom container CSS.\n* **For Developer F3 (Tracking View):** You will build inside the `/track` route. You can immediately use the `Input` search box, `Card` container, and `Status Badge Pill` to display ticket progress.\n* **For Developer F4 (Admin View):** You will build inside the `/admin` route. You will place your data table inside the pre-configured layout container and use the standardized `Status Badge Pill` and action `Buttons`.",
