      "text": "# Module B2: Ticket Ingestion \u0026 Keyword Routing Engine — Detailed Blueprint\n\n---\n\n## 1. Module Objective\nTo receive raw complaint submissions from the frontend, validate the user input, automatically assign the correct department using a deterministic keyword-matching dictionary, generate a unique tracking reference code, and save the ticket to the database.\n\nIn Version 1.0, this module provides the automated bridge between the user\u0027s plain text and the database schema built in Module B1, before machine learning is introduced in Version 2.0.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Ingestion Endpoint Specification\n\n* **Action / HTTP Method:** `POST`\n* **Route Path:** `/api/tickets`\n* **Access Level:** Public (No login required for complainants in V1.0)\n\n### 2.1 Expected Input Data\nThe endpoint expects three primary text inputs from the submission form:\n\n1. **Title:** Short summary of the complaint (e.g., *\"Water leakage in 2nd floor bathroom\"*).\n   * *Validation:* Mandatory; minimum 5 characters, maximum 150 characters.\n2. **Description:** Detailed explanation of the issue (e.g., *\"The pipe under the second sink has burst and is flooding the corridor\"*).\n   * *Validation:* Mandatory; minimum 10 characters, maximum 2,000 characters.\n3. **Location Description:** Building or area identifier (e.g., *\"Hostel Block C, Room 204\"*).\n   * *Validation:* Mandatory; minimum 3 characters, maximum 100 characters.\n\n### 2.2 Input Sanitization \u0026 Validation Rules\n* **Whitespace Cleaning:** Automatically strip leading and trailing spaces from all text fields.\n* **Empty Payload Rejection:** Reject the request immediately if any of the three required fields are missing, empty, or contain only spaces.\n* **Length Guardrails:** Return a clear validation error if text exceeds maximum character limits.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The Deterministic Keyword Routing Engine\n\nWhen a submission passes validation, the engine analyzes the combined text of the **Title** and **Description** to automatically select the responsible Department ID.\n\n```\n[Incoming Text: Title + Description]\n                 │\n                 ▼\n[Convert All Text to Lowercase]\n                 │\n                 ▼\n[Scan Against Department Keyword Dictionaries]\n    ├── Matches Electrical Keywords? ────► Assign Department ID: 1 (Electrical)\n    ├── Matches Plumbing Keywords?   ────► Assign Department ID: 2 (Plumbing)\n    ├── Matches Sanitation Keywords? ────► Assign Department ID: 3 (Sanitation)\n    ├── Matches Civil Keywords?      ────► Assign Department ID: 4 (Civil Maintenance)\n    └── Matches IT Keywords?         ────► Assign Department ID: 5 (IT Support)\n                 │\n        (No matches found OR conflict tie)\n                 │\n                 ▼\n    [Default Fallback] ──────────────────► Assign Department ID: 6 (General Administration)\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3.1 Department Keyword Dictionaries\n\nThe engine checks against the following pre-defined keyword lists:\n\n| Target Department | Department ID | Trigger Keywords |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Electrical** | **1** | `light`, `bulb`, `tube`, `wire`, `wiring`, `switch`, `socket`, `power`, `fan`, `fuse`, `shock`, `spark`, `blackout`, `electricity`, `voltage`, `ac`, `air conditioner`, `heater` |\n| **Plumbing** | **2** | `water`, `leak`, `leaking`, `pipe`, `tap`, `faucet`, `drain`, `flush`, `sink`, `toilet`, `washroom`, `sewage`, `clog`, `clogged`, `overflow`, `tank` |\n| **Sanitation \u0026 Housekeeping** | **3** | `garbage`, `trash`, `dustbin`, `dirty`, `clean`, `cleaning`, `smell`, `odor`, `stench`, `pest`, `rodent`, `rat`, `cockroach`, `sweep`, `sweeping`, `waste`, `litter` |\n| **Civil Maintenance** | **4** | `door`, `window`, `glass`, `table`, `chair`, `bench`, `lock`, `handle`, `wall`, `ceiling`, `plaster`, `paint`, `peeling`, `crack`, `roof`, `tiles`, `flooring`, `furniture` |\n| **IT \u0026 Network Support** | **5** | `wifi`, `wi-fi`, `internet`, `ethernet`, `lan`, `router`, `network`, `printer`, `projector`, `portal`, `server`, `cable` |\n| **General Administration** | **6** | *(Fallback)* Default when zero keywords match. |\n\n---\n\n### 3.2 Matching \u0026 Conflict Resolution Logic\n1. **Case-Insensitive Matching:** All input text is converted to lowercase before evaluation.\n2. **Frequency Scoring:** The engine counts how many keyword hits occur for each department. The department with the **highest number of keyword matches** wins.\n3. **Conflict / Tie-Breaker Rule:** If two departments tie with the same number of keyword hits (or if zero keywords match), the system automatically routes the ticket to **General Administration (ID: 6)** so staff can review it manually.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Public Tracking Code Generator\n\nEvery newly ingested ticket must receive a unique, human-friendly tracking reference code.\n\n* **Format Pattern:** `TICK-` prefix followed by 4 random uppercase alphanumeric characters (e.g., `TICK-4K89`, `TICK-92BW`).\n* **Uniqueness Guarantee:** The generator must check that the code does not already exist in the database before saving. If a collision occurs, it generates a new code automatically.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Output / Response Specification\n\n### 5.1 Success Response (Status: Created)\nWhen the ticket is successfully saved to the database, the endpoint immediately returns the following structured details to the frontend:\n\n* **Internal Ticket ID:** Unique database identifier.\n* **Public Tracking Code:** The generated reference code (e.g., `TICK-4K89`).\n* **Title:** The submitted complaint title.\n* **Assigned Department ID:** The numeric ID chosen by the routing engine.\n* **Assigned Department Name:** The readable name (e.g., *\"Plumbing\"*).\n* **Current Status:** `SUBMITTED`.\n* **Priority:** `MEDIUM` (Default for V1.0).\n* **Created Timestamp:** Exact UTC creation time.\n\n### 5.2 Error Responses\n* **Bad Request / Validation Error:** Triggered if any required text is missing, too short, or exceeds character limits. Returns a clear message explaining which field failed.\n* **Internal Server Error:** Triggered if database connectivity fails. Returns a safe error message without exposing database credentials.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Implementation Checklist for the Assigned Developer\n\n- [ ] Set up the `POST /api/tickets` request handler.\n- [ ] Implement text sanitization (trim whitespace, check min/max length limits).\n- [ ] Build the keyword dictionary mapping the 6 departments.\n- [ ] Implement the keyword scanning and frequency scoring algorithm.\n- [ ] Add the fallback assignment rule directing unmapped tickets to `General Administration (ID: 6)`.\n- [ ] Build the collision-safe `TICK-XXXX` tracking code generator.\n- [ ] Integrate with the database layer (Module B1) to execute the ticket insert.\n- [ ] Verify test submissions:\n  - Test *\"Flickering light bulb in lab\"* ➔ Correctly assigns **Electrical**.\n  - Test *\"Water leaking from broken sink tap\"* ➔ Correctly assigns **Plumbing**.\n  - Test *\"Unknown noisy issue in hallway\"* ➔ Correctly assigns **General Administration**.\n  - Test empty descriptions ➔ Correctly returns a validation error.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Handoff Contract (What Frontend F2 Receives)\n\nWhen the **Frontend Submission Form (Module F2)** sends a submission, Module B2 guarantees:\n1. A successful submission will return the **Tracking Code** and **Assigned Department Name** in the response.\n2. If the user enters invalid data, the API will return a descriptive error message that F2 can display directly beneath the form inputs.",
